      "side": 1,
      "message": "A dependency skipped here is never descended into, so a library with compiled syntax or a procedural macro, reached only through one that has neither, isn\u0027t remapped into the importer. Before, a pruned export imported every dependency recursively. `start_context` takes the same path.\n\nAt run time:\n\n```scheme\n;; w/h.sld: (define-library (w h) (import (scheme base)) (export h-stx)\n;;            (begin (define (h-stx) #\u0027(foo bar))))\n;; w/j.sld: (define-library (w j) (import (scheme base) (w h)) (export h-stx))\n;; w/k.sld: (define-library (w k) (import (scheme base) (w j)) (export h-stx))\n(import (scheme base) (scheme write) (w k))\n(display (syntax-\u003edatum (h-stx)))\n```\n\nThis prints `(foo bar)` on main. Here it raises `syntax: w/h.sld compiled no syntax at %syntax-build`.\n\nAt expansion time: a `syntax-case` macro in `(x h)` whose transformer calls `make-stx` from `(x h2)`, reached through a facade `(x m)` that holds only a `syntax-rules` macro. Main prints `from-h2`; here it fails with `h-mac: syntax: x/h2.sld compiled no syntax`. A macro library with helpers behind a facade is a common shape. Importing the middle library directly works, which is why the tests pass.\n\nWalk the skipped dependencies with a visited set: import any that runs code, and descend into the imports of any that doesn\u0027t. Alternatively, record per unit whether anything in its import closure runs code. Add the three-library case, at run time and at expansion time, to scaling.rs or the libraries tests.",
